</div><div id="Introduction" data-heading-anchor="1"><section id="sec1-energies-19-04893" type="intro"><h2 data-nested="1">  1. Introduction</h2><div class="html-p">The rapid development and deployment of large language models (LLMs) has increased attention to the computational and environmental costs of artificial intelligence. The concept of Green AI emphasizes that computational efficiency should be considered alongside predictive or generative performance when evaluating AI systems [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 1" reference-link="#B1-energies-19-04893">1</span>]. A systematic review of Green AI research similarly shows that energy monitoring, benchmarking, and deployment efficiency have become important research directions, although a substantial part of the earlier literature focused primarily on model training [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 2" reference-link="#B2-energies-19-04893">2</span>]. As generative models are deployed at scale, however, inference becomes a recurring operational workload and may therefore account for a substantial share of the energy associated with practical model use [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 3" reference-link="#B3-energies-19-04893">3</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 4" reference-link="#B4-energies-19-04893">4</span>]. Consequently, evaluating the energy efficiency of inference is increasingly relevant not only from an environmental perspective but also for hardware utilization, operating costs, and deployment scalability.</div><div class="html-p">Energy efficiency cannot be characterized adequately by GPU power alone. Power represents the instantaneous rate of energy consumption, whereas the energy required for an inference request depends jointly on power and execution time. A configuration operating at lower average power may therefore consume more total energy if its execution time increases sufficiently. Likewise, request-level energy and token-normalized energy address different questions: the former reflects the cost of completing a request, while the latter facilitates comparison of processing efficiency. Recent benchmarking work has consequently emphasized the importance of measuring both power and energy and of aligning measurements with individual phases of LLM inference [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 5" reference-link="#B5-energies-19-04893">5</span>].</div><div class="html-p">This distinction is particularly important because LLM inference consists of computationally different stages. During prompt processing, commonly referred to as the prefill phase, the input sequence is processed in parallel to construct the internal state required for generation. Autoregressive decoding subsequently generates tokens sequentially. Previous system-level characterization has shown that prompt processing tends to be relatively compute-intensive, whereas token generation is more strongly constrained by memory movement and exhibits different latency, utilization, and power characteristics [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 6" reference-link="#B6-energies-19-04893">6</span>]. The same optimization may therefore have substantially different effects in prefill and generation workloads. For this reason, energy measurements that aggregate the entire inference process without distinguishing workload composition can obscure phase-specific behavior.</div><div class="html-p">Quantization is one of the principal techniques used to reduce the computational and memory requirements of LLM inference. Post-training quantization methods such as GPTQ demonstrated that transformer weights can be represented at substantially reduced precision while maintaining useful model accuracy [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 7" reference-link="#B7-energies-19-04893">7</span>]. LLM.int8() introduced mixed-precision mechanisms that preserve higher precision for selected outlier dimensions while performing most matrix operations at 8-bit precision [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 8" reference-link="#B8-energies-19-04893">8</span>]. SmoothQuant extended efficient post-training quantization to 8-bit weights and activations [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 9" reference-link="#B9-energies-19-04893">9</span>], while AWQ demonstrated hardware-oriented low-bit weight-only quantization capable of reducing memory requirements and accelerating token generation [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 10" reference-link="#B10-energies-19-04893">10</span>]. More recent work has continued to investigate fine-grained and mixed low-precision representations explicitly from the perspective of energy-efficient LLM inference [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 11" reference-link="#B11-energies-19-04893">11</span>].</div><div class="html-p">The representation formats evaluated in this study should not be interpreted as uniform model-wide bit widths. Within the GGUF/llama.cpp ecosystem, Q8_0 uses block-wise quantization with 8-bit weight values and block-level scaling, whereas Q6_K and Q4_K belong to the K-quant family and use larger super-block structures together with quantized weights and associated scaling metadata. Q4_K_M is a model-level “mostly Q4_K” representation and may retain selected tensors in higher-precision quantized formats. Consequently, Q4_K_M, Q6_K, and Q8_0 are treated here as categorical weight-representation schemes rather than as exact 4-, 6-, and 8-bit representations of every model tensor. BF16 serves as the higher-precision reference format.</div><div class="html-p">Recent research has increasingly shifted from estimating the general computational footprint of AI toward direct measurement and characterization of inference energy. It was shown that AI energy consumption must be interpreted in relation to workload characteristics, hardware platform, and accelerator utilization. Kaur et al. examined the energy and emissions associated with LLM inference across application domains, highlighting the importance of workload-specific measurement [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 12" reference-link="#B12-energies-19-04893">12</span>]. At the accelerator level, Kang and Park demonstrated that measured power and energy efficiency depend on hardware characteristics and workload execution on heterogeneous AI accelerators [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 13" reference-link="#B13-energies-19-04893">13</span>], while earlier work by Kang et al. showed that GPU-based inference efficiency is closely linked to resource allocation and performance requirements [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 14" reference-link="#B14-energies-19-04893">14</span>]. Together, these studies reinforce the need to evaluate operational energy empirically rather than infer it solely from model size or theoretical computational complexity.</div><div class="html-p">Recent LLM-specific benchmarks further demonstrate that inference energy is highly sensitive to deployment conditions. Fernandez et al. found that the energy benefits of common inference optimizations vary substantially with workload geometry, software framework, GPU architecture, decoding strategy, and serving configuration [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 15" reference-link="#B15-energies-19-04893">15</span>]. Similarly, the ML.ENERGY Benchmark introduced systematic inference-energy measurements across multiple generative-AI models and tasks and demonstrated that energy consumption can vary considerably across otherwise functionally equivalent deployment configurations [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 16" reference-link="#B16-energies-19-04893">16</span>]. Oviedo et al. further showed that per-query energy depends strongly on output length, model and serving configuration, and hardware assumptions, particularly for reasoning-intensive and test-time-scaling workloads [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 17" reference-link="#B17-energies-19-04893">17</span>]. These findings indicate that energy efficiency is a system-level property emerging from the interaction among model characteristics, workload structure, software implementation, and hardware platform rather than from any single parameter in isolation.</div><div class="html-p">Hardware class represents an additional source of variation in inference-energy measurements. A high-end consumer GPU such as the RTX 4090 operates under different memory-capacity, memory-bandwidth, low-precision execution, and power-management constraints than server-oriented data-center accelerators. Data-center GPUs are designed for sustained accelerator workloads and typically provide substantially different memory systems and deployment characteristics. Cross-platform LLM inference studies have demonstrated that such hardware differences can materially affect both throughput and energy consumption [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 15" reference-link="#B15-energies-19-04893">15</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 16" reference-link="#B16-energies-19-04893">16</span>]. Accordingly, the present RTX 4090 results should be interpreted as a controlled consumer-GPU benchmark rather than as a quantitative proxy for data-center deployments.</div><div class="html-p">Existing quantization studies have predominantly focused on model size, memory footprint, accuracy retention, and inference speed [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 7" reference-link="#B7-energies-19-04893">7</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 8" reference-link="#B8-energies-19-04893">8</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 9" reference-link="#B9-energies-19-04893">9</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 10" reference-link="#B10-energies-19-04893">10</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 11" reference-link="#B11-energies-19-04893">11</span>], whereas the emerging literature on LLM energy has primarily examined differences among workloads, model architectures, serving configurations, and hardware platforms [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 12" reference-link="#B12-energies-19-04893">12</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 13" reference-link="#B13-energies-19-04893">13</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 14" reference-link="#B14-energies-19-04893">14</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 15" reference-link="#B15-energies-19-04893">15</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 16" reference-link="#B16-energies-19-04893">16</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 17" reference-link="#B17-energies-19-04893">17</span>]. Comparatively less attention has been devoted to isolating the effect of weight representation format on directly measured GPU energy while holding the hardware platform and inference backend constant. Limited controlled evidence is available on whether reductions in weight representation precision translate consistently into lower request- and token-level energy across different LLM families and across computationally distinct inference phases. This distinction is important because the energy benefit of quantization may depend not only on the amount of model data transferred but also on model-specific execution characteristics, dequantization overhead, GPU kernel efficiency, and the relative contribution of prompt processing and autoregressive decoding.</div><div class="html-p">The present study addresses this gap by evaluating four weight representation formats—Q4_K_M, Q6_K, Q8_0, and BF16—across four LLM families under a controlled single-GPU environment. Prefill-only and autoregressive generation workloads are analyzed separately, while energy measurements are considered together with throughput, GPU memory requirements, and mean GPU power. By holding the GPU platform and inference backend constant, the design enables within-family assessment of weight-representation effects with fewer hardware- and runtime-related confounders. Against this background, the study addresses the following research questions:</div><div class="html-p"><b>RQ1.</b> How does weight representation format affect GPU energy consumption and inference performance within the tested LLM families?</div><div class="html-p"><b>RQ2.</b> Are the effects of weight representation consistent between prefill-only and generation workloads, or do they depend on model family and workload phase?</div><div class="html-p"><b>RQ3.</b> Which model–precision configurations provide the most favorable joint trade-off among dynamic energy per token, generation throughput, GPU memory use, and mean GPU power?</div><div class="html-p">The contribution of the study is threefold. First, it provides a controlled comparison of 16 model–precision configurations executed on the same GPU and inference backend, enabling within-family evaluation of weight representation effects under a common experimental environment. Second, it explicitly separates prefill-only workloads from autoregressive generation and evaluates energy at both request and token levels, thereby accounting for the different computational characteristics of these inference phases. Third, it combines statistical inference with multi-criteria assessment using TOPSIS and Pareto dominance, allowing energy consumption, inference throughput, GPU memory requirements, and power demand to be considered jointly rather than in isolation.</div><div class="html-p">By integrating phase-specific energy measurements, within-family quantization comparisons, and multi-criteria evaluation within a single controlled experimental framework, the study complements existing research on LLM energy consumption and quantization with empirical evidence of the associated efficiency trade-offs.</div><div class="html-p">The novelty of the study therefore lies not in proposing a new quantization algorithm, but in isolating representation-format effects through a phase-aware, within-family GPU-energy benchmark under a fixed hardware and runtime environment, supported by factorial inference and complementary multi-criteria analysis.</div><div class="html-p">Importantly, all reported percentage improvements are specific to the NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU running llama.cpp with the CUDA backend and should not be interpreted as generalizable performance gains across other hardware architectures or inference frameworks.</div></section></div><div id="Materials_and_Methods" data-heading-anchor="1"><section id="sec2-energies-19-04893" type=""><h2 data-nested="1">  2. Materials and Methods</h2><section id="sec2dot1-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  2.1. Experimental Design and Benchmark Environment</h3><div class="html-p">The study was conducted as a controlled benchmark comparing the GPU energy consumption and inference performance of four large language model families: Gemma 4 E4B IT, Qwen3.5 4B, Nemotron 3 Nano 4B, and Ministral 3 3B Instruct. Each model family was evaluated using four weight representation formats: Q4_K_M, Q6_K, Q8_0, and BF16, resulting in a total of 16 model–precision configurations.</div><div class="html-p">Throughout the study, Q4_K_M, Q6_K, Q8_0, and BF16 were treated as categorical weight representation formats rather than as uniform bit-width representations of every model tensor. Accordingly, the term precision format used in the statistical analyses refers to the evaluated weight representation format.</div><div class="html-p">All experiments were performed on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 graphics processing unit (GPU) with a power limit of 450 W. The system used NVIDIA driver version 591.74 and a Windows x86_64 operating environment. Inference was performed using llama.cpp with the CUDA backend. All measurements were conducted on the same hardware and software platform. The experiments were performed using llama.cpp build 9950 (commit 961e4b26a) with CUDA 12.4. All model layers were offloaded to the GPU. The context size was set to 4096 tokens, with a batch size of 512 and a micro-batch size of 256. The key and value tensors of the KV cache were stored in FP16 format, and Flash Attention was enabled. These inference settings were kept constant across all model–precision configurations and benchmark scenarios. llama.cpp was selected because it provides native support for the evaluated GGUF weight-representation formats and CUDA GPU offloading within a single inference runtime. This allowed all model–format configurations to be evaluated using the same software backend, thereby avoiding framework changes as an additional experimental factor.</div></section><section id="sec2dot2-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  2.2. Benchmark Scenarios</h3><div class="html-p">Five benchmark scenarios were designed to distinguish prompt processing from autoregressive token generation:</div><div class="html-p"><ul class="html-bullet"><li><div class="html-p">PP-512—processing a 512-token prompt without the actual token generation phase;</div></li><li><div class="html-p">PP-1024—processing a 1024-token prompt without the actual token generation phase;</div></li><li><div class="html-p">TG-256—generating 256 tokens from a minimal single-token input;</div></li><li><div class="html-p">PG-512/256—processing a 512-token prompt and generating 256 output tokens;</div></li><li><div class="html-p">PG-2048/256—processing a 2048-token prompt and generating 256 output tokens.</div></li></ul></div><div class="html-p">One inference request was executed in each iteration. No continuous batching, concurrent requests, or request scheduling was used. This design was intended to provide a controlled baseline for attributing observed differences to the model–representation configuration rather than to serving-policy effects; it was not intended to reproduce a maximum-throughput production serving environment. Each model–precision–scenario configuration was evaluated in 10 independent repetitions, resulting in 10 observations per configuration within each benchmark scenario. The actual numbers of input and output tokens matched the target values specified for the corresponding scenario.</div><div class="html-p">The scenarios were analyzed separately because prompt processing and autoregressive token generation represent computationally different inference workloads and because the duration of the shortest prefill-only requests approached the temporal resolution of the GPU power telemetry.</div></section><section id="sec2dot3-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  2.3. GPU Power and Energy Measurement</h3><div class="html-p">GPU telemetry was recorded using the NVIDIA Management Library (NVML) with a nominal sampling interval of 50 ms. The same 50 ms polling interval was retained across all runs to maintain a consistent measurement protocol. Because the power telemetry exposed through NVML is itself updated at hardware- and driver-dependent intervals, increasing the software polling frequency does not necessarily yield proportionally finer or independent power measurements. The resulting telemetry was therefore not treated as high-resolution power tracing, particularly for short prefill-only requests. Immediately before each inference request, GPU idle power was determined while the corresponding model remained loaded.</div><div class="html-p">Total GPU energy for an inference request was calculated by integrating GPU power over the request duration (1):
