</div><div class="html-p">This ranking should be interpreted as a comparison of hardware performance in the tested environment. Without an independent measurement of response quality, the results do not allow us to conclude that the configuration ranked first is the best model in terms of overall usability or the quality of the generated text.</div></section></div><div id="Discussion" data-heading-anchor="1"><section id="sec4-energies-19-04893" type="discussion"><h2 data-nested="1">  4. Discussion</h2><section id="sec4dot1-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.1. Principal Findings</h3><div class="html-p">The results demonstrate that the relationship between weight precision and GPU energy efficiency is strongly dependent on workload phase and model architecture. The clearest pattern was observed during autoregressive generation. Across all three generation scenarios, model family, precision format, and their interaction significantly affected the primary endpoints, with very large effect sizes for both model family and precision. The significant interaction is particularly important because it indicates that the benefit associated with a given weight representation cannot be interpreted independently of the model on which it is applied.</div><div class="html-p">Within all four tested model families, Q4_K_M was substantially more energy-efficient and faster than BF16 during generation. Depending on the model and scenario, Q4_K_M reduced mean dynamic energy per processed token by 63.6–85.7% and increased generation throughput by 85.2–160.2%. These improvements were statistically robust across the planned Q4_K_M–BF16 comparisons. The magnitude of the effect therefore indicates that, in the tested environment, aggressive weight quantization was not merely a memory-saving mechanism; it translated into substantial reductions in GPU energy required for token generation.</div><div class="html-p">The results were less uniform in the prefill-only workloads. No statistically supported effects of model family, precision, or their interaction were detected for total request energy, prompt throughput, or execution time in PP-512. In PP-1024, precision affected total energy and average GPU power, but corresponding effects on prompt throughput and execution time were not significant. These findings should not be interpreted as evidence that the configurations are equivalent during prefill. Rather, they indicate that statistically supported differences were limited under the adopted workload and measurement design.</div><div class="html-p">Consequently, the answer to the question posed in the article title is conditional. Lower precision produced very large and consistent benefits when Q4_K_M was compared with BF16 during the tested generation workloads, but the same strength of evidence was not observed for short prefill-only requests. Nominal precision therefore cannot be treated as a universal predictor of request-level GPU energy across all inference phases.</div></section><section id="sec4dot2-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.2. Why the Effect of Quantization Depends on the Workload Phase</h3><div class="html-p">The difference between prefill and generation is consistent with established characterizations of LLM inference. Prompt processing and autoregressive decoding have different computational profiles: prefill is relatively compute-intensive, whereas token generation is more strongly influenced by repeated weight access and memory traffic [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 6" reference-link="#B6-energies-19-04893">6</span>]. Weight quantization directly reduces the amount of model data that must be stored and transferred and can therefore be particularly advantageous in workloads where memory movement limits performance. AWQ similarly identifies memory size and memory bandwidth as key constraints in LLM serving and demonstrates substantial generation speedups from hardware-oriented weight-only quantization [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 10" reference-link="#B10-energies-19-04893">10</span>].</div><div class="html-p">This provides a plausible mechanism for the strong Q4_K_M advantage observed during generation. A smaller weight representation may reduce memory traffic sufficiently to increase token throughput, shorten execution time, and thereby lower energy per token even if the instantaneous GPU power does not decrease proportionally. Importantly, the present experiment did not directly decompose energy into arithmetic, memory-transfer, cache, or dequantization components. The mechanism should therefore be interpreted as being consistent with previous system-level findings rather than as a directly measured causal decomposition.</div><div class="html-p">The prefill results require additional caution because the requests were short relative to the nominal 50 ms NVML sampling interval. The study appropriately refrained from interpreting dynamically baseline-corrected energy for these cases. This measurement constraint reduces the ability to distinguish small differences between configurations during short prompt-processing workloads. Therefore, the absence of statistically significant differences in PP-512 should not be interpreted as proof that quantization has no influence on prefill energy.</div><div class="html-p">The results also demonstrate why power and energy should not be used interchangeably. A configuration may have higher or similar mean power while still consuming less energy if it finishes the request substantially faster. Conversely, reducing mean GPU power alone does not guarantee improved energy efficiency. For deployment decisions, energy per completed request or per token should therefore be considered together with throughput and latency.</div></section><section id="sec4dot3-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.3. Model Family and Quantization Format Interact</h3><div class="html-p">The very large main effects of precision were accompanied by substantial model × precision interactions. This result reinforces the conclusion that quantization should be evaluated as a property of a complete model–runtime–hardware configuration rather than as an isolated numerical parameter. GPTQ, LLM.int8(), SmoothQuant, and AWQ already demonstrate that quantization performance depends strongly on how weights, activations, scaling factors, and outliers are represented and processed [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 7" reference-link="#B7-energies-19-04893">7</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 8" reference-link="#B8-energies-19-04893">8</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 9" reference-link="#B9-energies-19-04893">9</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 10" reference-link="#B10-energies-19-04893">10</span>]. Recent low-precision research similarly emphasizes that the detailed numerical representation and hardware execution strategy can matter as much as nominal bit width itself [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 11" reference-link="#B11-energies-19-04893">11</span>].</div><div class="html-p">The present results extend this observation to GPU energy consumption. Although Q4_K_M outperformed BF16 within every tested model family during generation, the magnitude of the reduction differed substantially among models. For Ministral 3 3B Instruct, Q4_K_M reduced dynamic energy per token by 84.0–85.7% relative to BF16 while increasing throughput by 105.2–160.2%. This was one of the strongest effects observed in the benchmark.</div><div class="html-p">The cross-model comparison between the two strongest Q4_K_M configurations further illustrates the architectural component. Ministral–Q4_K_M used 37.1–42.6% less dynamic energy per token than Nemotron–Q4_K_M across the three generation scenarios and achieved approximately 10.9–12.1% higher mean throughput. At the same time, Nemotron required substantially less GPU memory. Thus, even after the same nominal quantization format was selected, the two model families occupied different positions in the energy–performance–memory design space.</div><div class="html-p">Cross-model comparisons must nevertheless be interpreted as comparisons of complete systems. The models differ in architecture, parameterization, tokenizer behavior, and potentially other implementation characteristics. They should therefore not be interpreted as a controlled estimate of the causal effect of model architecture alone. The strongest evidence regarding precision comes from within-family comparisons, where the underlying model is held constant.</div></section><section id="sec4dot4-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.4. Multi-Criteria Efficiency: TOPSIS and Pareto Analysis</h3><div class="html-p">Considering a single metric would obscure several practically important trade-offs. Ministral 3 3B Instruct–Q4_K_M achieved the lowest dynamic energy per token and the highest generation throughput among the leading configurations and consequently ranked first in TOPSIS in all three generation scenarios, with scores of 0.981–0.982. Nemotron 3 Nano 4B–Q4_K_M ranked second in each scenario, with scores of 0.917–0.932. These two configurations were also the only solutions located on the Pareto frontier in all three generation scenarios.</div><div class="html-p">The Pareto result is particularly useful because it does not require assigning explicit weights to optimization criteria. Neither configuration completely dominated the other: Ministral offered superior energy efficiency and throughput, whereas Nemotron required approximately 4.9 GiB of GPU memory compared with approximately 6.4 GiB for Ministral. The appropriate configuration therefore depends on the deployment constraint.</div><div class="html-p">Under the adopted TOPSIS weights, dynamic energy per token and throughput accounted jointly for 80% of the decision weight. Consequently, the lower energy requirement and higher throughput of Ministral outweighed its approximately 30% larger GPU-memory footprint. If memory capacity were the dominant operational constraint, however, Nemotron would represent the more favorable option.</div><div class="html-p">This finding illustrates an important deployment principle: the most energy-efficient configuration is not necessarily the optimal configuration for every system. GPU memory may determine whether several model instances can be hosted simultaneously, while throughput may determine service capacity and energy may dominate operating costs. Multi-criteria analysis therefore complements single-metric benchmarking, but its results must always be interpreted in relation to the selected criteria and weights.</div></section><section id="sec4dot5-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.5. Relation to Previous Research</h3><div class="html-p">The substantial performance improvements associated with low-precision weights are consistent with earlier quantization research. GPTQ showed that 3- and 4-bit post-training weight quantization can substantially reduce LLM storage and enable faster inference while retaining useful accuracy [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 7" reference-link="#B7-energies-19-04893">7</span>]. LLM.int8() demonstrated the value of mixed-precision treatment of outliers [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 8" reference-link="#B8-energies-19-04893">8</span>], while SmoothQuant and AWQ reported substantial memory and performance benefits from hardware-oriented quantization strategies [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 9" reference-link="#B9-energies-19-04893">9</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 10" reference-link="#B10-energies-19-04893">10</span>]. BlockDialect further demonstrates continuing interest in explicitly energy-efficient low-precision inference [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 11" reference-link="#B11-energies-19-04893">11</span>].</div><div class="html-p">The present study differs from much of this literature by focusing directly on measured GPU energy rather than treating memory reduction or inference speed as proxies for energy efficiency. This distinction is important because faster inference does not mathematically guarantee lower energy consumption unless the accompanying power profile is also considered.</div><div class="html-p">The present findings are also consistent with recent empirical studies showing that LLM inference energy is strongly dependent on workload and deployment conditions. Fernandez et al. demonstrated that the energy impact of inference optimizations varies substantially across workload geometries, software stacks, and GPU platforms [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 15" reference-link="#B15-energies-19-04893">15</span>], while the ML.ENERGY Benchmark showed large differences in inference energy among model, task, and deployment configurations [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 16" reference-link="#B16-energies-19-04893">16</span>]. Similarly, Oviedo et al. reported a strong dependence of per-query energy on token generation requirements and serving assumptions [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 17" reference-link="#B17-energies-19-04893">17</span>]. The present results extend this body of evidence by demonstrating that, even when the GPU platform and inference backend are held constant, weight representation format interacts strongly with model family and workload phase.</div><div class="html-p">The findings also complement recent work published in <span class="html-italic">Energies</span>. Kaur et al. emphasized the importance of workload-specific assessment when estimating the operational energy and emissions associated with LLM inference [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 12" reference-link="#B12-energies-19-04893">12</span>], whereas Kang and Park demonstrated that accelerator-level energy efficiency depends on the characteristics of the execution platform [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 13" reference-link="#B13-energies-19-04893">13</span>]. The present study contributes a more narrowly controlled perspective by examining the energy–performance consequences of weight representation under a fixed single-GPU environment. This design does not replace broader system-level benchmarking, but allows the contribution of quantization format to be evaluated with fewer hardware-related confounders.</div><div class="html-p">The findings also align with the broader Green AI principle that computational efficiency should be measured explicitly rather than inferred from model size or theoretical operation counts [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 1" reference-link="#B1-energies-19-04893">1</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 2" reference-link="#B2-energies-19-04893">2</span>]. The marked differences observed between prefill and generation are consistent with recent calls for phase-aware inference benchmarking and for the joint reporting of request-level and token-normalized energy metrics [<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 5" reference-link="#B5-energies-19-04893">5</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 15" reference-link="#B15-energies-19-04893">15</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 16" reference-link="#B16-energies-19-04893">16</span>,<span tabindex="0" role="link" class="html-reference" aria-label="Reference 17" reference-link="#B17-energies-19-04893">17</span>].</div><div class="html-p">At the same time, the present results do not establish the environmental footprint of the tested models in a full life-cycle sense. The measurements concern operational GPU energy only. They do not include embodied hardware impacts, data-center cooling, power-delivery overhead, CPU and system energy, or the carbon intensity of the electricity supply. Consequently, the results should be described as GPU energy-efficiency findings rather than direct estimates of carbon emissions or comprehensive environmental sustainability.</div></section><section id="sec4dot6-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.6. Interpretation of Token-Normalized Energy</h3><div class="html-p">An additional methodological point concerns comparisons of dynamic energy per processed token between workload scenarios. In TG-256, nearly all processed tokens belong to the autoregressive generation phase, whereas the PG-512/256 and PG-2048/256 scenarios contain progressively larger proportions of prompt tokens. Because prompt processing and token generation have different computational characteristics, the denominator in the J/token metric represents a different mixture of work in each scenario.</div><div class="html-p">Accordingly, the decrease in absolute J/token values observed when moving from TG-256 to workloads containing longer prompts should not be interpreted simply as evidence that longer prompts intrinsically improve energy efficiency. Token-normalized energy is most directly comparable between configurations evaluated within the same scenario. Request-level energy, energy per output token, throughput, and workload composition should be considered alongside J/token when comparisons are made across scenarios.</div><div class="html-p">This distinction is particularly relevant in LLM energy benchmarking because normalization can strongly affect conclusions. A metric that is appropriate for comparing two implementations under the same workload may become misleading when prompt length, generated-token count, or batching structure changes substantially.</div></section><section id="sec4dot7-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.7. Practical Implications</h3><div class="html-p">Several practical implications follow from the results. First, weight quantization should be benchmarked on the intended model–GPU–runtime combination rather than selected solely based on nominal precision. The substantial model × precision interaction indicates that the magnitude of the energy benefit is architecture-dependent.</div><div class="html-p">Second, optimization should be workload-aware. Systems dominated by autoregressive generation may obtain very large benefits from low-bit weight representations, whereas the gains for short prefill workloads may be smaller or more difficult to measure reliably. Applications with different prompt-to-output ratios may therefore favor different deployment configurations.</div><div class="html-p">Third, throughput and energy should be evaluated jointly. In the present benchmark, the most energy-efficient generation configurations were also among the fastest, indicating that higher throughput and lower energy per token need not represent conflicting objectives. Nevertheless, GPU memory introduces an additional constraint that can change the preferred deployment choice.</div><div class="html-p">Finally, energy benchmarking should report sufficient context to make results reproducible: the specific model artifact, quantization format, inference backend, hardware platform, software version, workload composition, energy-measurement method, and normalization denominator are all relevant to interpretation. Energy values should not be generalized across hardware or inference engines without validation.</div></section><section id="sec4dot8-energies-19-04893" type=""><h3 class="html-italic" data-nested="2">  4.8. Limitations and Future Work</h3><div class="html-p">The study has several limitations. First, all experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090. The results therefore characterize the tested consumer-GPU environment and should not be generalized directly to other NVIDIA architectures, data-center accelerators, or non-NVIDIA hardware. Different GPUs provide different low-precision arithmetic capabilities, memory bandwidth, cache hierarchies, and power-management behavior.</div><div class="html-p">Second, inference was performed using llama.cpp with a CUDA backend in a Windows environment. Quantization performance is sensitive to kernel implementation and runtime optimization; consequently, the numerical results may differ under alternative frameworks such as vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, or other CUDA/Linux serving stacks.</div><div class="html-p">Third, the energy measurements represent GPU energy derived from NVML telemetry rather than whole-system electricity consumption. CPU activity, host memory, storage, power-supply losses, and cooling overhead were outside the measurement boundary. The results should therefore be interpreted specifically as GPU-level operational energy.</div><div class="html-p">Fourth, the 50 ms nominal telemetry interval limits the temporal resolution available for very short requests. This is particularly relevant to the prefill-only scenarios and is one reason why dynamic baseline-corrected energy was not interpreted for those workloads.</div><div class="html-p">Fifth, one request was executed per iteration. The benchmark therefore does not reproduce production environments involving continuous batching, concurrent users, request scheduling, or high GPU utilization. Such operating modes may alter both throughput and energy efficiency.</div><div class="html-p">Sixth, the evaluated prompts extended to 2048 input tokens. Long-context inference may exhibit different relationships among attention computation, KV-cache activity, memory traffic, and quantization. Future work should therefore include longer contexts and varying input/output ratios.</div><div class="html-p">Seventh, response quality was not measured. The TOPSIS and Pareto analyses consequently evaluate hardware-resource efficiency rather than overall model utility. A configuration that minimizes energy per token may be less attractive if quantization materially reduces task accuracy, reasoning performance, factuality, or other quality dimensions. Future work should combine energy measurements with within-model quality-retention metrics and construct quality–energy Pareto frontiers.</div><div class="html-p">Eighth, TOPSIS rankings depend on the selected weights. Although Pareto analysis provides a weight-independent complement, future work should include a sensitivity analysis to determine whether rankings remain stable under alternative priorities for energy, throughput, memory, and power.</div><div class="html-p">Although the evaluated formats can be ordered approximately by nominal precision, Q4_K_M, Q6_K, and Q8_0 are distinct block-wise representation schemes rather than points on a uniform continuous bit-width scale. For this reason, the revised analysis evaluates the full set of formats through within-model pairwise comparisons instead of assuming a linear relationship between nominal bit width and energy consumption. Future work should extend this analysis across additional quantization schemes, hardware architectures, and inference backends.</div></section></section></div><div id="Conclusions" data-heading-anchor="1"><section id="5Conclusions" type=""><h2 data-nested="1">   5. Conclusions</h2><div class="html-p">This study examined whether lower weight precision necessarily translates into lower GPU energy consumption during LLM inference. In the tested NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 and llama.cpp/CUDA environment, the strongest conclusion concerns autoregressive generation: Q4_K_M substantially outperformed BF16 within all four evaluated model families, reducing dynamic energy per token by 63.6–85.7% while simultaneously increasing generation throughput by 85.2–160.2%. These effects were statistically robust and demonstrate that aggressive weight quantization can provide major energy–performance benefits during generation.</div><div class="html-p">However, the results do not justify treating nominal precision as a universal or architecture-independent predictor of energy use. The significant model × precision interactions show that the magnitude of the benefit depends on the model family, while the substantially weaker findings in the short prefill-only scenarios demonstrate that the effect is also workload-phase-dependent.</div><div class="html-p">Among the evaluated generation configurations, Ministral 3 3B Instruct–Q4_K_M provided the most favorable combination of energy efficiency and throughput and ranked first in TOPSIS across all three scenarios. Nemotron 3 Nano 4B–Q4_K_M consistently ranked second and offered a substantially smaller GPU-memory footprint. Both configurations belonged to the Pareto frontier, illustrating that the preferred deployment option depends on the relative importance of energy, throughput, memory capacity, and power.</div><div class="html-p">Overall, the results indicate that quantization formats should be evaluated empirically on the intended model–hardware–runtime combination. Deployment decisions should use request-level and token-level energy together with throughput, latency, power, and memory requirements rather than relying on nominal bit width alone. Because model quality was not measured, the identified hardware-efficiency rankings should not be interpreted as rankings of overall model quality or usability. The numerical effect sizes reported here should therefore be interpreted as specific to the tested RTX 4090–llama.cpp/CUDA environment and should be independently validated before being extrapolated to data-center accelerators, alternative GPU architectures, or production serving frameworks.</div></section></div><section><div id="Supplementary_Materials" data-heading-anchor="1"><section id="app1-energies-19-04893" type=""><h2 data-nested="1">  Supplementary Materials</h2><div class="html-p">The following supporting information can be downloaded at: <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/article/10.3390/en19204893/s1" target="_blank">https://www.mdpi.com/article/10.3390/en19204893/s1</a>, Table S1: Detailed pairwise results, including mean differences, confidence intervals, effect size, and Hold-adjusted <span class="html-italic">p</span>-values.</div></section></div></section><div id="Author_Contributions" data-heading-anchor="1"><section class="html-notes"><h2>Author Contributions</h2><div class="html-p">Conceptualization, P.T., J.L., E.K., A.L. and R.P.; methodology, P.T. and J.L.; software, P.T., E.K. and R.P.; validation, P.T., J.L. and A.L.; formal analysis, J.L., P.T. and A.L.; investigation, P.T., J.L., E.K. and A.L.; resources, R.P.; data curation, E.K. and P.T.; writing—original draft preparation, J.L., P.T. and A.L.; writing—review and editing, J.L., E.K., A.L. and R.P.; visualization, P.T.; supervision, J.L. and P.T.; project administration, J.L. and R.P.; funding acquisition, P.T., J.L., E.K., A.L. and R.P. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.</div></section></div><div id="Funding" data-heading-anchor="1"><section class="html-notes"><h2>Funding</h2><div class="html-p">This research received no external funding.</div></section></div><div id="Data_Availability_Statement" data-heading-anchor="1"><section class="html-notes"><h2>Data Availability Statement</h2><div class="html-p">The data supporting the findin